Noticed another issue when I try to use "commonjs mode". When I import a `.js` file, that itself imports another `.js` file via an alias, that also fails (I've added an example in the `commonjs` branch, [link to commit over here](https://github.com/piotrpalek/playwright-issue/commit/00edac4e560635b2b6e6cda791c6c9b6354633b0)). Which is I think a common pattern in projects that are migrating to typescript, or have lots of legacy code / use webpack aliases.

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1474323/194299447-97eff961-2ffa-4a12-9645-ab46c6f4db95.png)

edit: Now that I'm thinking about it, if there's a `tsconfig`, shouldn't all files always go through the transform? I'm wondering why only `.ts/.tsx` files trigger that mode. For eg. in our case we use aliases for non-ts files as well, but I imagine there could be other cases that don't come to mind now, that would benefit from it as well. The same goes for "buildins", if I let everything go through the typescript transform (buildins, .js, .ts, everything) my project loads fine.

Basically in [transform.ts](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/main/packages/playwright-test/src/transform.ts#L95) what I've changed is from this:

```typescript
export function resolveHook(filename: string, specifier: string): string | undefined {
  if (builtins.has(specifier))
    return;
  const isTypeScript = filename.endsWith('.ts') || filename.endsWith('.tsx');
  const tsconfig = isTypeScript ? loadAndValidateTsconfigForFile(filename) : undefined;
  if (tsconfig && !isRelativeSpecifier(specifier)) {
  ...
```

to this:

```typescript
export function resolveHook(filename: string, specifier: string): string | undefined {
  const tsconfig = isTypeScript ? loadAndValidateTsconfigForFile(filename) : undefined;
  if (tsconfig && !isRelativeSpecifier(specifier)) {
  ...
```
